{"abstract":"This volume discusses the transformational role that carbon – both as a concept and as a distinct set of material forms and effects – has come to play in social and cultural life. As a proxy for greenhouse gas emission data, carbon has grown to become a phenomenon that can no longer be accounted for solely within the technoscientific vocabulary of climate scientists. The Cultural Complexity of Carbon examines the extent to which our knowledge of carbon affects the way that human beings relate to each other and to the climate and/or the environment. It draws on case studies from a diverse range of topics including peatland restoration, religion and energy systems to explore questions that have so far been under-explored in the current literature. These questions include whether the recognition of carbon’s role in climate change leads to an incremental adaptation of lifestyles or to cultural or existential transformations, but also more concretely how carbon is made meaningful, and how these meanings are attached to ideals of cultural change or continuity. Spanning multiple perspectives and disciplinary positions, this volume provides a go-to point for the next generation of ethnographic studies of carbon and climate change. It cuts across what has hitherto been largely separate literatures in anthropology, geography and sociology to provide a meta-level orientation to how contemporary narratives of the role of carbon are being told. By addressing the intimate social and cultural changes that stem from humanity’s involvement with its natural and climatic resources, this volume is of interest to students and scholars of climate change within the social sciences and environmental humanities.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Dalsgaard, Steffen","Lautrup, Andy","Lippert, Ingmar","Schyberg, Katinka Amalie"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818245"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Goethe University Frankfurt"],"keyword":["Built environment","Environment and Sustainability","Social sciences"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the cultural complexity of carbon","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/edit/10.4324/9781003478669/cultural-complexity-carbon-steffen-dalsgaard-andy-lautrup-ingmar-lippert-katinka-schyberg","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818245","ids":["itu-7fd3f908-8982-4110-aaa6-081fdc6c99f0"],"keyword":["Built Environment","Environment and Sustainability","Social Sciences"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-34C1AAFA34993C5FAA1EA54B4CED51F6"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Climate IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"de","key":"goethe university frankfurt","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Goethe University Frankfurt","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Steffen","key":"dalsgaardpau","last":"Dalsgaard","locid":"bc7a60f7-45a0-401f-813f-6b4aa700c849","name":"Dalsgaard, Steffen","orcid":"0000-0003-3712-6988","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Andy","key":"lautruppau","last":"Lautrup","name":"Lautrup, Andy","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Ingmar","key":"lippertpau","last":"Lippert","name":"Lippert, Ingmar","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Katinka Amalie","key":"schybergpau","last":"Schyberg","name":"Schyberg, Katinka Amalie","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9781003478669"],"pages":"209","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7fd3f908-8982-4110-aaa6-081fdc6c99f0"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Cultural Complexity of Carbon","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818245","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This chapter sheds light on what happened as carbon was introduced as a resource to think with, evaluate by and act upon in the Danish People’s Church. The chapter specifically attends to how the prospect of calculating and displaying carbon footprints played a significant role in the negotiations of State-Church relations in Denmark in the years 2020 to 2022 when this Scandinavian country – including its national Church – tried to live up to the reduction goals outlined in its 2021 national climate law. In the chapter, I show what happened as the Church both resisted and was drawn into the logic of carbon footprinting and how the prospect of such footprinting influenced the relationship between the State and the Church – a relationship that, for the most part, goes unnoticed but which became a matter of public scrutinization and contestation as the need for a green transition in Denmark also became a Church responsibility. The chapter shows how accounting for carbon emissions was used in the service of achieving what is otherwise deemed impossible: namely, to speak on behalf of the entire Church without compromising either its internal “religious freedom” (trosfrihed) or its ethos of “spaciousness” (rummelighed) – some of the key values underpinning the organization of the Church. I argue that the method of calculating and displaying carbon footprints as a response to climate change offered the Church a way to simultaneously claim responsibility while not having to compromise on the principle of decentralization.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Schyberg, Katinka Amalie"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-829055"],"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the footprint of anarchy counting carbon in the church","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-829055","identifier":[{"id":"105003355618","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-678bd42a-d11c-49b5-8060-860453b4571a"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-79C4ECD24FD3F61147BDAA3E09E75A22"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Sociology, Department of Sociology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Katinka Amalie","key":"schybergpau","last":"Schyberg","locid":"01c87a8a-4ce0-4edd-927c-6a0e338a5ea3","name":"Schyberg, Katinka Amalie","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"pages":"86-109","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","subtitle":"Green Transformations in Contemporary Society","title":"The Cultural Complexity of Carbon"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-678bd42a-d11c-49b5-8060-860453b4571a"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The footprint of anarchy: counting carbon in the Church","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-829055","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This paper is about attempts made by environmentally concerned pastors within the Danish People’s Church to articulate a so-called “eco-theology” fit for a time of climate crisis. In the paper, I describe how re-describing the God-world relation as one marked by ‘deep incarnation’ enables the pastors to establish the grounds for extending the commandment to love one’s neighbor to also encompass non-human others. I argue that as the pastors are trying to bring the world and God, the human and the nonhuman closer together, in order to, ultimately, enact Christian love, they are dealing with a problem that pertain to discerning and articulating the right relational configuration, or, as I suggest calling it, the optimal distance, for care. A problem, I suggest, that is also at the locus of anthropology in regard to what kind of relation ought to be between the anthropologist and her Others (Robbins 2013) - including nonhuman ones. Through a contrapuntal reading (Reinhardt 2015) of eco-theological and anthropological engagements with human-nonhuman relations, the paper draws attention to how both these intellectual traditions entail engaging with the question of what makes up a relation of care and how such a relation hinges on calibrating the optimal distance between the subject and object of care.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Schyberg, Katinka Amalie"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-829729"],"event":{"end":"2025-06-23","place":"Santa Barbara , us","start":"2025-06-21","title":"The Society for the Anthropology of Religion (SAR) Biennial Conference","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2025-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"love thy nature an ethnography of eco theological efforts within the danish church","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-829729","ids":["ku-b85b835f-c083-491f-b5ff-4893e358b4ea"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FB28228A381F3C1498DFF2BBEB64B837"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Sociology, Department of Sociology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Katinka Amalie","key":"schybergpau","last":"Schyberg","locid":"01c87a8a-4ce0-4edd-927c-6a0e338a5ea3","name":"Schyberg, Katinka Amalie","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-b85b835f-c083-491f-b5ff-4893e358b4ea"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Love thy Nature: an ethnography of eco-theological efforts within the Danish Church","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-829729","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"This thesis explores what was at stake for church actors as the demand and desire to respond to climate change travelled in and through the Danish People’s Church in the years 2020 to 2022. Based on ethnographic fieldwork, the thesis depicts a period in the life of this national church in which the problem of climate change became a shared matter of concern. What kind of matter it was, however, was disputed and the thesis lays out some of the efforts church actors made to define as well as respond to it. As such, the thesis describes the practices and perspectives of various actors in the church – pastors, deans, gardeners, project managers, and energy consultants – as they engage in “greening” the Church. It shows how grappling with the problem of climate crisis actualized other, already established problems within the Church – such as that of an unresolved state-church relation, a distant God, and an ambivalent Protestant relationship to the material. The thesis shows that engaging with climate change incites church actors to question, undo, but also retain some of the key distinctions that are understood to organize the Church and Christianity. Hence, in the thesis it is demonstrated how distinctions such as those between religion and politics, the material and the immaterial, the human and the non-human, and God and the world, are questioned, negotiated, and the relation between the terms potentially reconfigured in the course of church actors’ engagements with climate change. In order to describe the particular dynamics of relating and separating, retaining unity and difference within this process of reconfiguration - this undoing and redrawing of boundaries that it is argued takes place in the various greening efforts within the Church - the concept of counterpoint is evoked.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Schyberg, Katinka Amalie"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776414"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"church and climate change in counterpoint an ethnography of environmental engagements within the danish people s church","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109068922/Final_Thesis_Katinka_Schyberg.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776414","ids":["itu-740b0952-cbdb-4a0a-b4fa-8d87281ef400"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-8FDF96171789DC22C1EE31D6CD9B8D11"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109068922/Final_Thesis_Katinka_Schyberg.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Katinka Amalie","key":"schybergpau","last":"Schyberg","locid":"8aaf5ea6-92c2-4780-bba3-cdc171315e0b","name":"Schyberg, Katinka Amalie","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779495265"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"229","pages":"271","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-740b0952-cbdb-4a0a-b4fa-8d87281ef400"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Church and Climate Change in Counterpoint : An Ethnography of Environmental Engagements within the Danish People's Church","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776414","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
